WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
- My first guest is a
hell of a talk show host,

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.850
a producer, a comedian, an activist

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.930
and a six-time New York
Time's best-selling author,

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:08.950
not too shabby.

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.797
Her new book is called "Life
Will Be the Death of Me

00:00:11.797 --> 00:00:14.330
"and you, too!" (laughs)

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:15.163
It's awesome.

00:00:15.163 --> 00:00:18.280
I always love seeing her,
Chelsea Handler, y'all.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:19.680
Hi!
- Hi, hi!

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:20.940
- How are you?

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:21.810
- I'm good, honey.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:22.930
How are you doing?

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.340
- I love your house.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:26.990
- I do, too!

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:28.160
- Oh, my gosh!

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.030
But anyway, life has certainly changed

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.073
since we saw each other last.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.598
I don't know, I guess everybody's been,

00:00:35.598 --> 00:00:37.363
in your own relative way,

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:39.530
whatever's happening in your life.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:42.270
So how have you been handling
not only the quarantine

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.640
but there's the Black Lives Matter.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:44.950
There's just a lot going on right now.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.040
How are you handling everything?

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.980
- Well, the quarantine
started with two weeks,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.560
I did my own quarantine.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:55.160
And then, my sister came
down with her three kids.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.540
And so, they invaded my house.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:00:59.900
What I realized when quarantine happened

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.040
is people start to get worried
about me, 'cause I'm single.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:02.873
They're like, "Oh, Chelsea.

00:01:02.873 --> 00:01:05.370
"She's alone, she's single."

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.040
- I get jealous! (laughs)

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.777
- I'm like, "This is exactly
what I've been hoping for."

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:13.990
I don't need anybody to look after me.

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.610
I mean, I do need people to
work things around my house,

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:18.510
'cause I'm useless in that department.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.380
So that would be helpful if
I were gonna get a boyfriend,

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:22.880
if he were good at that stuff.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:27.100
So they invaded my house and
they were here for two months.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.710
And so, I had a full
house of kids and a mom

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:32.688
and we'd had dinners
and played board games.

00:01:32.688 --> 00:01:34.858
It was sweet.
- Oh, I love board games.

00:01:34.858 --> 00:01:36.936
- I don't.
- I do.

00:01:36.936 --> 00:01:39.850
- It was sweet to have that family vibe,

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.950
because obviously I'm single
and very loud about that.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:44.140
And I'm childless.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.060
But also in quarantine, I've learned

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.167
that being single and childless is like,

00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:50.746
"You know, I made a
couple of smart moves."

00:01:50.746 --> 00:01:52.829
(laughs)

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.380
- Coming from a person who
spent most of the quarantine

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.820
with three children, it was a lot.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:01.610
I wanna ask, because
we're now finally having

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:04.130
a real conversation about white privilege.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:06.200
I actually watch a lot of things you do,

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.870
'cause I'm a fan of yours.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:09.857
But last year, you did
a documentary called,

00:02:09.857 --> 00:02:11.727
"Hello Privilege. It's Me, Chelsea."

00:02:12.909 --> 00:02:14.950
Way ahead of the curve on that one.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.030
But what inspired you to do that

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:18.773
and what did you learn from it?

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.100
- I wrote my last book, "Life
Will Be the Death of Me"

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.180
which was about going to therapy

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.370
for the first time in my life.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:29.730
And when I did go to therapy
for the first time in my life,

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.210
after Trump was elected in 2016,

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.600
I had a lot of anger
and I had a lot of rage

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.140
about him being elected
and I didn't understand.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.330
And so, I had to do
some really deep digging

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:43.570
to A, what was going on with
me and my reaction to that

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:46.150
and why I couldn't calmly discuss it

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.850
instead of with my veins
popping out of my neck

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:51.330
and tweeting while I'm sleeping.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.820
Trying to actually get
a hold of my emotions

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.680
and be in a place where I'm
responding, not reacting.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.090
And through that work and going to therapy

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:04.630
seriously for a while,
it was just obvious to me

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:07.140
how I had failed to look around and see

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.870
how the color of my skin
has been such a benefit

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.120
to my own success.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.410
So I was very interested
in doing a documentary

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:16.520
about finding out, what
does white privilege mean?

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:17.607
Because to me, it just meant,

00:03:17.607 --> 00:03:18.927
"Oh, people who go to Harvard

00:03:18.927 --> 00:03:23.170
"or go to those Ivy League
schools or the Rockefellers."

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:24.840
I thought that was white privilege,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.173
when I started that documentary.

00:03:28.363 --> 00:03:29.760
And then you learn that, no,

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.090
you don't have to go to
an Ivy League school.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:33.410
Your family doesn't have to be wealthy

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:35.730
for it to be a privilege
to have white skin.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:37.590
You are benefiting from a system

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.230
that is hurting other people.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.180
As long as people are hurting,

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.750
then there is a group of people

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:44.930
that are benefiting from that.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:46.490
That's just the way it works.

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:48.250
And so, it opened my eyes so much

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.767
and I just loved that
film and I had to say it.

00:03:50.767 --> 00:03:52.670
And it was a film to white people.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:54.487
To say, "Listen, I know
these conversations

00:03:54.487 --> 00:03:56.797
"are uncomfortable, but
we should be having them

00:03:56.797 --> 00:03:59.090
"because we have a
problem in this country."

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:01.060
- And without you being uncomfortable,

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.370
I don't think change will even occur.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:06.600
So you're a part of a
protest in Los Angeles,

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.760
or protests, rather in Los Angeles.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.010
So how did that experience make you feel?

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.480
Because I have friends that have gone out

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.500
and there have been very
different experiences,

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.650
depending on which
protest and where you were

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:19.540
and what happened.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:21.490
So how was your experience?

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:22.643
- I went to a couple.

00:04:23.694 --> 00:04:26.750
One was organized by the LGBTQ
community in West Hollywood.

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:29.411
So it was the most organized protest

00:04:29.411 --> 00:04:31.350
I've ever been to in my life.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:32.183
- I love those people.

00:04:32.183 --> 00:04:33.157
Organization!
- Yeah, I'm like,

00:04:33.157 --> 00:04:34.017
"Oh my God, you guys.

00:04:34.017 --> 00:04:35.801
"You guys should arrange all the marches!"

00:04:35.801 --> 00:04:38.550
(laughs)

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.480
It was totally peaceful, it was beautiful

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.313
and it was nice.

00:04:41.313 --> 00:04:45.320
I did go to the big LA
one previous to that.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.950
That was in a big park and
then it took to the streets.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:49.420
And then, that was one of the protests

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:52.910
that got a little bit out
of control at nighttime

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:54.870
and it turned into
looting and all of that.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.470
But I had left earlier on.

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:59.580
As soon as the social
distancing became impossible

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:03.967
and irresponsible, I felt
like, "Okay, let me go.

00:05:03.967 --> 00:05:05.007
"I've been here."

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:08.180
How did it make me feel?

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:09.410
It made me feel great.

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.910
I wish I had gone out every
single day to protest.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.420
And I just feel like we have,

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:16.260
whatever every person is doing,

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.160
and some people aren't doing anything yet

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:19.710
because they're not sure what to do,

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.330
but we can all do a little bit more.

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:23.930
We can just do a little bit more

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:25.730
to broaden our awareness and learn

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:28.000
a little bit more than we already know.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:28.833
- Absolutely.

00:05:28.833 --> 00:05:30.870
And even if you can't go protest,

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.320
having those hard
conversations with your family.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:35.230
And depending on where you're from,

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:36.530
especially where I'm from,

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.020
those can be very difficult conversations.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:41.540
But they're important for all of us

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.890
if we actually wanna see something happen

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:47.090
and not repeat the same vicious cycle.

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:49.140
But one of the things I
found interesting about you,

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.110
because I do think that
you're really open,

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:55.470
is that you've said earlier
you were against therapy.

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.023
And then, you actually ended up doing it.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:00.600
Was a lot of it because dealing

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:01.650
with the death of your brother?

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:05.230
'Cause I imagine that
had to be huge for you.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:06.270
- Yeah.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.993
I had this crazy reaction to Trump.

00:06:10.548 --> 00:06:13.690
And I was like, "This is
unmanageable, my outrage."

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:16.750
And people were seeing me coming
and they're like (groans).

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:21.060
And then, he explained to me over time

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:24.850
and over a lot of work,
that Trump being elected

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:27.630
was really the trigger of the
only other time in my life

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:29.730
where things were out of control.

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:31.690
Even if you look at me as
someone who's out of control,

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:32.907
it's like, "I've got my act together.

00:06:32.907 --> 00:06:35.810
"I know what's up and what's
down and what's happening."

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:38.373
It felt like the world was not in control.

00:06:39.585 --> 00:06:42.033
And he took me back to my childhood.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:45.780
I thought I was too smart to
ever even have to go there.

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:50.270
- Actually, 100% no and go to therapy.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:52.540
So, yes. (laughs)
- Right, so you know.

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:54.110
- You think you're past
it and you know it.

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:55.530
And although you know the situation

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:57.550
and although you think you figured it out,

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:00.910
you just don't realize how
much PTSD comes into play

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:02.910
when one thing triggers you like that.

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:03.763
- Absolutely.

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:10.240
He just took me back to
my childhood and built

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:12.770
on my relationship with
my brother and reminded me

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:15.300
of why there was so much pain inside of me

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.850
that presented as anger.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:20.330
Underneath the anger is always something

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:21.470
hurtful.
- Deeper.

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:22.303
- Yeah.

00:07:22.303 --> 00:07:23.536
And so, I was acting out.

00:07:23.536 --> 00:07:27.530
It worked for me for most of
my life, not addressing it.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:28.363
But eventually,

00:07:28.363 --> 00:07:29.950
it catches up to you.
- Compartmentalizing

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:31.350
only works for so long.

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.080
- Eventually, your life is broken

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:34.980
and there's no way to
walk without crutches.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:37.070
And then, eventually you
have to go to the doctor

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:39.227
and be like, "Can you
patch me up the real way

00:07:39.227 --> 00:07:40.500
"and the right way?"

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.550
So I had great therapy experience

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:44.500
and that's what my whole book is about,

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:47.210
is about therapy and I.

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:48.077
I thought, "Oh, my God.

00:07:48.077 --> 00:07:49.787
"If this is how I'm dealing with something

00:07:49.787 --> 00:07:51.447
"that happened to me when I was nine,

00:07:51.447 --> 00:07:54.313
"imagine how many other people
have the same experience.

00:07:56.337 --> 00:07:58.947
"I've overshared, my entire
career is about oversharing.

00:07:58.947 --> 00:08:02.267
"So why not overshare this beautiful thing

00:08:02.267 --> 00:08:04.890
"that can actually help heal people?"

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:06.413
It helped heal me, for sure.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:10.393
- I'm called an over-sharer as well.

00:08:11.977 --> 00:08:14.780
At some point, I think
that's also why people

00:08:15.752 --> 00:08:16.990
tend to believe you when you say something

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:18.430
or tend to believe me when I say something

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:20.810
'cause it's always coming
from a really honest place

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:23.063
and there are no walls
up in that instance.

00:08:24.582 --> 00:08:26.050
I don't know, sometimes
I think oversharing

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:27.360
can be a good thing.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:28.640
All right, well thank
you so much, Chelsea.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:30.087
Y'all have to get Chelsea's book,

00:08:30.087 --> 00:08:31.590
"Life Will Be the Death of Me."

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:34.490
It's available in paperback on June 23rd.

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:36.850
Support your independent
bookstores and support Chelsea.

00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:37.730
Obviously, great author.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:39.843
She's had a billion number one books.

